ped to MW grades.\",\n      \"score\": 0.5,\n      \"strengths\": \"Recognizes that higher molecular weight generally improves mechanical performance.\",\n      \"areas_for_improvement\": \"Correct the mechanism (entanglement, not crosslinking); mention wear resistance/toughness; connect UHMWPE to joint prostheses and LDPE/LLDPE to flexible tubing.\",\n      \"breakdown\": \"- Relationship MW\u2192properties: 0.5/1.0 (generic, partly inaccurate)\\n- Link to medical applications by MW grade: 0.0/1.0\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_id\": \"3\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Step 1: Student defines degree of polymerization (number of repeating units) and provides DP = (molecular weight of polymer)/(molecular weight of monomer). Step 2: States that increasing number of monomers (higher DP) increases molecular weight and leads to better mechanical properties, but does not specify tensile strength or durability. Step 3: Does not mention melting point, chain entanglement, or intermolecular forces.\",\n      \"score\": 1.0,\n      \"strengths\": \"Correct definition and formula for DP; correctly notes that higher DP generally improves properties.\",\n      \"areas_for_improvement\": \"Explicitly state which properties improve (tensile strength, durability) and note reasons (entanglement/intermolecular forces) and effects like higher melting point.\",\n      \"breakdown\": \"- Definition of DP: 0.5/0.5\\n- Impact on mechanical properties (tensile strength/durability): 0.5/1.0 (too generic)\\n- Other aspects (melting point/intermolecular forces): 0.0/0.5\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_id\": \"4\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Step 1: Student describes linear polymers as straight chains and assigns high tensile strength and high density; example HDPE\u2014acceptable. Step 2: For cross-linked polymers, the student describes monomers 'attach to the straight chain' (branching) and lists low density and less tensile strength, with example LDPE\u2014this is incorrect (LDPE is branched, not cross\u2011linked). Step 3: Rubric requires correct structural descriptions and property comparison (rigidity/thermal stability). The cross-linked description and properties are wrong, so comparative properties are not satisfied.\",\n      \"score\": 0.5,\n      \"strengths\": \"Reasonable description and properties for linear polymers.\",\n      \"areas_for_improvement\": \"Correctly define cross-linked networks (chains interconnected by covalent bonds) and their properties (rigidity, high strength, thermal stability); avoid using LDPE as a cross-linked example.\",\n      \"breakdown\": \"- Structure of linear polymers: 0.5/0.5\\n- Structure of cross-linked polymers: 0.0/0.5 (misidentified as branching)\\n- Property comparison (density, tensile strength, rigidity, thermal stability): 0.0/1.0 (mostly incorrect)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_id\": \"5\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Step 1: Student lists natural polymers as highly biocompatible/recognized and fast degrading; synthetic polymers have good/adjustable mechanical properties and modifiable degradation. Step 2: Provides implications: notes immunogenicity risk (for natural), moderate mechanics for natural, and lower biocompatibility/slower degradation for synthetics. Step 3: Rubric expects two key differences (biocompatibility/recognition; mechanics/modifiability) and implications for biomedical use. These are mostly present, though some points are vague and miss specifics (e.g., lack of cell-adhesion signals or inflammatory degradation products for synthetics).\",\n      \"score\": 1.5,\n      \"strengths\": \"Clearly contrasts biocompatibility/recognition and mechanical tunability; includes examples.\",\n      \"areas_for_improvement\": \"State explicit biomedical implications (e.g., natural: good cell interactions but weaker mechanics; synthetic: controlled degradation and strength but lack cell cues/possible inflammation).\",\n      \"breakdown\": \"- Difference 1 (biocompatibility/recognition): 0.5/0.5\\n- Difference 2 (mechanics/modification): 0.5/0.5\\n- Implications for applications: 0.5/1.0 (present but not specific)\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall_feedback\": \"All questions were attempted. Q1 effectively contrasted PLA and PLGA with appropriate biomedical applications, though explicit mechanical-property discussion was brief. Q2 lacked the required linkage between molecular weight, wear resistance/toughness, and specific medical uses (UHMWPE vs LDPE) and included an incorrect mechanism. Q3 correctly defined DP but needed clearer naming of which properties improve and why. Q4 mischaracterized cross-linked polymers (confusing with branching) and thus failed the property comparison. Q5 captured the main differences between natural and synthetic polymers but should articulate clearer application implications. Focus on tying properties to specific biomedical uses and ensuring definitions (e.g., cross-linking, entanglement) are accurate.\"\n}"
