ction and correctly linked it to joint implants. They did not clearly describe applications for lower-MW PE (e.g., LDPE for flexible items/tubing) and did not explicitly mention toughness, though implied by strength/wear comments.\",\n      \"score\": 1.6,\n      \"strengths\": \"Correct trend of properties with molecular weight; accurate identification of UHMWPE for joint prostheses with reasons (wear, friction).\",\n      \"areas_for_improvement\": \"State that lower-MW variants (e.g., LDPE) are more flexible/less strong and give a medical use. Mention toughness explicitly.\",\n      \"breakdown\": \"- Relationship: higher MW \u2192 higher tensile/wear resistance (addressed).\\n- Applications: UHMWPE for joints (addressed); LDPE/HDPE applications not provided (missing).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_id\": \"3\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"In the \u2018Degree of polymerization\u2019 section, student defined DP as number of monomer units and provided the formula DP = molecular weight of polymer / molecular weight of monomer (correct). They explained that higher DP leads to increased compactness/density, which yields higher tensile strength, durability, and wear resistance. They did not mention melting point changes or the role of intermolecular forces/entanglements explicitly.\",\n      \"score\": 1.5,\n      \"strengths\": \"Clear definition and correct formula; correctly linked higher DP to greater strength and durability.\",\n      \"areas_for_improvement\": \"Add that higher DP increases chain entanglement/intermolecular forces and often raises melting point; avoid equating DP strictly with density.\",\n      \"breakdown\": \"- Definition of DP (0.5/0.5): provided.\\n- Impact on strength/durability (\u22481/1): described.\\n- Other aspects (0/0.5): melting point/intermolecular forces not mentioned.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_id\": \"4\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The linear vs cross\u2011linked comparison table describes linear chains vs covalently cross\u2011linked networks. Properties contrasted: linear are flexible/moldable, easier to soften; cross\u2011linked are rigid/harder/brittle and thermally more stable (hard to denature/soften). Included simple sketches and examples (PLA/PE vs epoxy/Bakelite). Meets structural descriptions and property comparison requirements.\",\n      \"score\": 2.0,\n      \"strengths\": \"Accurate structural descriptions, appropriate property contrasts, examples and sketches.\",\n      \"areas_for_improvement\": \"Minor terminology: use \u2018soften/melt\u2019 instead of \u2018denaturation\u2019; density comments could be qualified.\",\n      \"breakdown\": \"- Structure of linear polymers (0.5): correct.\\n- Structure of cross\u2011linked polymers (0.5): correct.\\n- Property comparison (1.0): flexibility vs rigidity, thermal stability, strength\u2014addressed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_id\": \"5\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"In the natural vs synthetic section, student noted origins and gave examples. They stated natural polymers provide cell adhesion/differentiation and have high biocompatibility/bioresorbability\u2014capturing biological recognition. For synthetic, they wrote that cell proliferation is \u2018not possible\u2019 (overstated) and that biocompatibility/bioresorbability are not high. They did not discuss mechanical strength advantages of synthetic polymers or tunable degradation, nor provide clear biomedical implications for each beyond recognition/biocompatibility.\",\n      \"score\": 1.0,\n      \"strengths\": \"Correctly identified biological recognition/biocompatibility advantage of natural polymers; provided examples.\",\n      \"areas_for_improvement\": \"State that synthetic polymers offer higher/measurable mechanical strength and tunable degradation; provide balanced biomedical implications (e.g., natural: good cell interactions but weaker mechanics; synthetic: strong and tailorable but may lack cell signals/produce inflammatory by\u2011products). Avoid